  {
   "id": "A1",
   "text": "Spacetime is a smooth 4-D Lorentzian manifold (signature -,+,+,+); at every point p a local inertial frame exists (equivalence principle), so g_ab = diag(-1,1,1,1) at p and local Rindler horizons can be built."
  },
  {
   "id": "A2",
   "text": "Local Rindler horizon: through p pick a small spacelike 2-surface P; its past-directed null normals generate a null congruence, affinely parametrised by lambda (lambda = 0 at P, lambda < 0 to the past), tangent k^a, hypersurface-orthogonal so the twist omega vanishes."
  },
  {
   "id": "A3",
   "text": "Local equilibrium / instantaneous stationarity at p: the expansion theta(p) = 0 and the shear sigma(p) = 0 (Jacobson: P chosen so both vanish to first order; this choice is what makes the state equilibrium)."
  },
  {
   "id": "A4",
   "text": "Entropy is proportional to horizon area: dS = eta * dA with eta a universal constant (motivated by vacuum entanglement across the horizon with a UV cutoff; eta is NOT fixed by the argument)."
  },
  {
   "id": "A5",
   "text": "Unruh temperature: the vacuum seen by the boost observer is thermal at T = hbar kappa/(2 pi) (c = k_B = 1), kappa the acceleration of the orbit where |chi| = 1 (Unruh 1976; Bisognano-Wichmann)."
  },
  {
   "id": "A6",
   "text": "Heat = boost-energy flux of matter across the horizon: dQ = int_H T_ab chi^a dSigma^b with chi^a = -kappa lambda k^a (approximate boost Killing field vanishing at P) and dSigma^b = k^b dlambda dA. Quantum fluctuations of T_ab neglected; T_ab classical, symmetric."
  },
  {
   "id": "A7",
   "text": "Clausius relation dQ = T dS holds for ALL local Rindler horizons through ALL points p, i.e. for every null k."
  },
  {
   "id": "A8",
   "text": "Locality / leading order: the horizon patch is small compared with the curvature radius, so R_kk and T_kk are evaluated at p and only the leading power of the patch size eps is kept."
  },
  {
   "id": "A9",
   "text": "Matter stress-energy is conserved: nabla^a T_ab = 0."
  },
  {
   "id": "A10",
   "text": "Spacetime (the domain of f) is connected, so a scalar with vanishing gradient is a constant."
  },
  {
   "id": "A11",
   "text": "Identification: the coupling 2 pi/(hbar eta) is NAMED 8 pi G; G is taken from measurement, the derivation then predicts eta = 1/(4 hbar G), it does not predict G."
  },
